  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nThere is a growing sense of complacency as central Illinois \nremains under the influence of atmospheric blocking. Over the \nlast 7 to 10 days, this persistent pattern has continuously \nsteered frontal systems away from the area. As a result, the \nregion is undergoing one of its warmest Octobers on record, with\nPeoria, Lincoln, and Springfield currently ranking 3rd, 5th, \nand 10th warmest, respectively.\n\nIt will be more of the same through the front half of this \nweek. The western trough is forecast to open and lift across the\nRockies tonight, then pivot across the Northern Plains by \nMonday night. While this trough will be effective in \nde-amplifying or flattening the upper-level blocking pattern, \nit will not be quite enough to break it down completely. The \nmain surface front will remain displaced to our north, and the \nnet effect will be another warm and dry day on Monday with highs\nwell into the 70s despite increasing high clouds.\n\nAn auxiliary shortwave trough moves out of the Southern Plains \ntoward the Ozarks on Tuesday. Although it delivers minor \nsynoptic lift along with a slight surge of Gulf moisture to the \ncentral U.S. Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, our local \narea stays positioned on the western edge of dry surface high \npressure. We therefore anticipate any high-based showers to \nremain off to our north and west over Iowa.\n\nFollowing this midweek disturbance, upper-level ridging quickly\nbuilds back in, promoting dry weather and unseasonable warmth \nthrough at least Thursday afternoon. Because a northern-stream \nshortwave tries to push a backdoor cold front into central \nIllinois on Thursday, we do carry low probabilities of rain. \nHigh-based, scattered showers remain possible near and north of \nInterstate 74 as the front sags southward, though both blended \n(NBM) and global ensemble (LREF Mean) guidance suggest rain is \nunlikely (10% to 30% chance). Any light precipitation that does \nland will fail to meaningfully impact the ongoing extended dry \nstretch.\n\nOtherwise, the surface front is forecast to retreat back \nnorthward by Friday, ensuring high temperatures will stay in the\nupper 70s to near 80 degrees. This continued thermal ridge will\ncomfortably lock in what stands as one of the warmest Octobers \non record.\n\nNevertheless, model guidance does indicate a clear, large-scale\npattern shift taking shape heading into next weekend and the \nstart of November. Even with spatial and temporal variances \namong synoptic features, confidence is high that upper-level \nflow across the central U.S. will become significantly faster \nand more progressive, transitioning the area toward cooler, \nwetter, and windier conditions.",
